# performance.mdc — Performance Evaluation & Promotion (Phased Model)

## 1) Core Principle
Performance tracking exists to support **selection under uncertainty**, not vanity metrics.
All strategy instances are treated as investment candidates competing for attention and capital.

## 2) Performance Phases
The system operates in explicit phases. Each phase adds constraints and authority.

### Phase 1 — Visibility & Triage
Goal: Observe all instances and eliminate obvious losers.

- All instances are visible in a single list.
- Metrics are descriptive only.
- No instance may be promoted based on Phase 1 alone.

Mandatory metrics:
- realized_pnl (net)
- unrealized_pnl
- total_pnl
- trade_count
- win_rate
- max_drawdown
- runtime_duration

Instances below minimum evidence thresholds MUST be labeled `INSUFFICIENT_DATA`.

---

### Phase 2 — Structured Comparison & Candidate Selection
Goal: Identify strategies with plausible, risk-aware edge.

Additions:
- `evidence_tier` ∈ {INSUFFICIENT_DATA, TRACKING, CANDIDATE}
- Composite score (profit rewarded, drawdown and churn penalized)
- Recent vs full-window performance comparison

Promotion to `CANDIDATE` requires:
- minimum trades and runtime met
- positive net total_pnl
- max_drawdown below configured limit
- no data_quality BAD flags

Promotion is manual or explicitly approved.

---

### Phase 3 — Shadowing & Live Readiness
Goal: Validate behavior under real execution constraints.

Additions:
- `SHADOW` tier
- real market data and order generation
- fill quality, timing drift, churn metrics

Rules:
- No LIVE promotion without SHADOW validation
- Any divergence between expected and observed behavior triggers demotion

---

### Phase 4 — Portfolio Optimization & Automation
Goal: Maximize portfolio-level profit under risk constraints.

Additions:
- capital allocation per strategy
- correlation and redundancy analysis
- regime-aware activation
- automated promotion/demotion within strict bounds

Automation is allowed only in this phase.

## 3) Non-Negotiables
- Never promote based on PnL alone
- Never rank strategies without minimum evidence
- Always show drawdown next to profit
- Costs and slippage assumptions must be explicit
